{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgX7PYhkvW5dyd6HsDgxz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FCxRUXdJ87eLd95bXCSoRm","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/invisible_formatting_effects_20261029_120001_qr78st90/README.md","content":"# Invisible Formatting Effects\n\nDo meaning-preserving, (near-)invisible formatting changes such as trailing spaces, CRLF line endings, double spaces,\nno-break spaces, curly quotes, en/em dashes and ellipsis characters change what an LLM outputs? We ran 11 open models\n(0.5B–14.8B; Qwen2.5, Qwen3 and Llama-3.x) on 3 classification tasks (SST-2, AG News, BoolQ; 1,200 items) and 2\ncode-generation tasks (HumanEval, MBPP; 464 problems). Each item ran under a canonical prompt, a byte-identical re-run\n(the noise floor) and 10 formatting perturbations, all with greedy decoding: about 220k generations in total.\n\n**Full write-up: [REPORT.md](REPORT.md)**\n\n## Key findings\nSee REPORT.md §1 for the numbers. In short:\n- Invisible formatting changes do flip classification labels, well above the byte-identical noise floor (≈0–0.2%).\n  The mean flip rate is in the low single digits for ≥3B models and higher for <3B models.\n- **Whitespace hurts more than punctuation**, the reverse of the hypothesis. Double spaces, no-break spaces, CRLF and\n  trailing spaces cause roughly 2–5× more flips than curly quotes or Unicode dashes. Divergence tracks how many prompt\n  tokens the perturbation rewrites, but even a single extra newline token flips some labels.\n- Small models (<3B) are more sensitive than larger ones.\n- Generated code text changes very often (tens of %), but much of that is cosmetic. Pass/fail flips are much rarer,\n  and the byte-identical re-run already changes the code text a few % of the time.\n- Net accuracy barely moves: flips go in both directions and land mostly on items the model gets wrong or is unsure about.\n- Placebo check: Llama-3.x chat templates strip trailing newlines, so the token sequence is unchanged and the flip rate is exactly 0.\n\n## Reproduce\n```bash\nuv venv --python 3.12 && source .venv/bin/activate && uv sync      # vllm 0.32, pandas, statsmodels, ...\nexport NUMPY_MADVISE_HUGEPAGE=0 HF_HOME=/data/transformers\npython src/build_prompts.py                                         # data/items.jsonl, data/prompts.jsonl (19,968 prompts)\nCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 python src/worker.py logs/queue.txt 16       # local models (one key per line in the queue file)\npython src/gen_server.py 48                                         # Qwen3-8B via an OpenAI-compatible vLLM server on :8011\npython src/tok_effect.py                                            # does each perturbation change the token sequence?\npython src/score.py                                                 # label parsing + sandboxed unit-test execution\npython src/analyze.py                                               # tables (results/tables), figures (figures/), stats.json\n```\n\n## Layout\n```\nplanning.md             research plan (motivation, hypotheses, analysis plan)\nsrc/perturb.py          the 10 perturbations (+ code-aware application: prose-only edits touch docstrings/comments only)\nsrc/build_prompts.py    datasets -> prompts (canonical, control, perturbations)\nsrc/worker.py           in-process vLLM worker for shared GPUs (greedy decoding, flock per model)\nsrc/gen_server.py       generation through the local Qwen3-8B server\nsrc/tok_effect.py       chat template + tokenizer: token edit size per perturbation\nsrc/score.py            label parsing, code extraction, test execution (cached by program hash)\nsrc/analyze.py          divergence metrics, bootstrap CIs, McNemar tests, GEE models, figures\nresults/gens/*.jsonl    raw generations, one file per model\nresults/tables/         all CSV tables;  results/stats.json  summary statistics\nfigures/                heatmaps, per-condition bars, size trends\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":704,"cache_read_input_tokens":185146,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":704},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ca68dff7-3fed-493e-b883-6ab7358c4498","uuid":"c8828dd4-72d7-4b06-b9d5-81b6c8c997b3","timestamp":"2026-10-29T19:51:45.438Z","request_id":"req_011CgX7PYWLx4EHyP7HU4zPS","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01FCxRUXdJ87eLd95bXCSoRm":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/invisible_formatting_effects_20261029_120001_qr78st90/README.md","content":"# Invisible Formatting Effects\n\nDo meaning-preserving, (near-)invisible formatting changes such as trailing spaces, CRLF line endings, double spaces,\nno-break spaces, curly quotes, en/em dashes and ellipsis characters change what an LLM outputs? We ran 11 open models\n(0.5B–14.8B; Qwen2.5, Qwen3 and Llama-3.x) on 3 classification tasks (SST-2, AG News, BoolQ; 1,200 items) and 2\ncode-generation tasks (HumanEval, MBPP; 464 problems). Each item ran under a canonical prompt, a byte-identical re-run\n(the noise floor) and 10 formatting perturbations, all with greedy decoding: about 220k generations in total.\n\n**Full write-up: [REPORT.md](REPORT.md)**\n\n## Key findings\nSee REPORT.md §1 for the numbers. In short:\n- Invisible formatting changes do flip classification labels, well above the byte-identical noise floor (≈0–0.2%).\n  The mean flip rate is in the low single digits for ≥3B models and higher for <3B models.\n- **Whitespace hurts more than punctuation**, the reverse of the hypothesis. Double spaces, no-break spaces, CRLF and\n  trailing spaces cause roughly 2–5× more flips than curly quotes or Unicode dashes. Divergence tracks how many prompt\n  tokens the perturbation rewrites, but even a single extra newline token flips some labels.\n- Small models (<3B) are more sensitive than larger ones.\n- Generated code text changes very often (tens of %), but much of that is cosmetic. Pass/fail flips are much rarer,\n  and the byte-identical re-run already changes the code text a few % of the time.\n- Net accuracy barely moves: flips go in both directions and land mostly on items the model gets wrong or is unsure about.\n- Placebo check: Llama-3.x chat templates strip trailing newlines, so the token sequence is unchanged and the flip rate is exactly 0.\n\n## Reproduce\n```bash\nuv venv --python 3.12 && source .venv/bin/activate && uv sync      # vllm 0.32, pandas, statsmodels, ...\nexport NUMPY_MADVISE_HUGEPAGE=0 HF_HOME=/data/transformers\npython src/build_prompts.py                                         # data/items.jsonl, data/prompts.jsonl (19,968 prompts)\nCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 python src/worker.py logs/queue.txt 16       # local models (one key per line in the queue file)\npython src/gen_server.py 48                                         # Qwen3-8B via an OpenAI-compatible vLLM server on :8011\npython src/tok_effect.py                                            # does each perturbation change the token sequence?\npython src/score.py                                                 # label parsing + sandboxed unit-test execution\npython src/analyze.py                                               # tables (results/tables), figures (figures/), stats.json\n```\n\n## Layout\n```\nplanning.md             research plan (motivation, hypotheses, analysis plan)\nsrc/perturb.py          the 10 perturbations (+ code-aware application: prose-only edits touch docstrings/comments only)\nsrc/build_prompts.py    datasets -> prompts (canonical, control, perturbations)\nsrc/worker.py           in-process vLLM worker for shared GPUs (greedy decoding, flock per model)\nsrc/gen_server.py       generation through the local Qwen3-8B server\nsrc/tok_effect.py       chat template + tokenizer: token edit size per perturbation\nsrc/score.py            label parsing, code extraction, test execution (cached by program hash)\nsrc/analyze.py          divergence metrics, bootstrap CIs, McNemar tests, GEE models, figures\nresults/gens/*.jsonl    raw generations, one file per model\nresults/tables/         all CSV tables;  results/stats.json  summary statistics\nfigures/                heatmaps, per-condition bars, size trends\n```\n"}}}
