{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgXF79u33Wd5yMbRvu85j","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NVi9VPTVLGC45std57jHYg","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/invisible_formatting_effects_20261029_120001_qr78st90; python3 - << 'EOF'\np='README.md'; s=open(p).read()\na=s.index('## Key findings'); b=s.index('## Reproduce')\ns=s[:a]+'''## Key findings\n- One invisible formatting change flips **4.9%** of classification labels on average (95% CI 4.6–5.1%), against\n  **0.02%** for a byte-identical re-run. The edit is significant in 80 of 110 model × perturbation cells (Holm-corrected).\n- **Whitespace hurts more than punctuation**, the reverse of the hypothesis. Whitespace flips 7.1% of labels and\n  punctuation 2.2%, and whitespace is worse in every model. Part of the reason is that double spaces and NBSPs re-tokenise\n  every word, but whitespace stays worse at a matched number of changed tokens.\n- Models under 3B parameters flip **2.2×** as often as ≥3B models on classification (CI 2.0–2.4; 2.9× within\n  Qwen2.5). On code, pass/fail flips show no size effect.\n- Code text changes for **36%** of problems (identical re-run: 6%), but pass/fail changes for only **5.1%**, about the\n  same as classification label flips.\n- Net accuracy barely moves (mostly within ±1 pp): flips go both ways and land mostly on items the model gets wrong.\n  Placebo check: Llama chat templates strip trailing newlines, so the token sequence is unchanged and the flip rate is exactly 0.\n\n'''+s[b:]\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\np='planning.md'; s=open(p).read()\ns+='''\n## Deviations from the plan (recorded after execution)\n- No API keys on this host, so all models are open weights. Final set: 11 models, Qwen2.5 0.5–14B AWQ, Llama-3.2-1B/3B,\n  Llama-3.1-8B-AWQ, Qwen3-0.6B/4B-AWQ, and Qwen3-8B via the local vLLM server. Qwen3-0.6B/4B were added mid-run to get a\n  third same-family size series.\n- Final perturbations: whitespace = trailing_space, crlf, double_space, trailing_newlines, nbsp (classification) or\n  tabs (code); punctuation = curly_quotes, curly_apostrophe, unicode_dash, ellipsis_char, punct_all.\n  `ellipsis_char` applies to few items. Unchanged prompts are excluded.\n- Added a replication run (Qwen2.5-0.5B in a separate engine): all outputs were byte-identical.\n- Mixed models: GEE logistic (exchangeable, clustered by item) instead of a mixed-effects GLM.\n'''\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ncat pyproject.toml | sed -n '/dependencies/,/]/p'","description":"Update README findings and planning deviations"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":741,"cache_read_input_tokens":241875,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":741},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ca68dff7-3fed-493e-b883-6ab7358c4498","uuid":"ca1d00d3-35cc-4476-b6f8-c865bc54a8e1","timestamp":"2026-10-29T21:32:52.943Z","request_id":"req_011CgXF79fQCBvz1hzhcLffi","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01NVi9VPTVLGC45std57jHYg":{"command":"cd /data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/invisible_formatting_effects_20261029_120001_qr78st90; python3 - << 'EOF'\np='README.md'; s=open(p).read()\na=s.index('## Key findings'); b=s.index('## Reproduce')\ns=s[:a]+'''## Key findings\n- One invisible formatting change flips **4.9%** of classification labels on average (95% CI 4.6–5.1%), against\n  **0.02%** for a byte-identical re-run. The edit is significant in 80 of 110 model × perturbation cells (Holm-corrected).\n- **Whitespace hurts more than punctuation**, the reverse of the hypothesis. Whitespace flips 7.1% of labels and\n  punctuation 2.2%, and whitespace is worse in every model. Part of the reason is that double spaces and NBSPs re-tokenise\n  every word, but whitespace stays worse at a matched number of changed tokens.\n- Models under 3B parameters flip **2.2×** as often as ≥3B models on classification (CI 2.0–2.4; 2.9× within\n  Qwen2.5). On code, pass/fail flips show no size effect.\n- Code text changes for **36%** of problems (identical re-run: 6%), but pass/fail changes for only **5.1%**, about the\n  same as classification label flips.\n- Net accuracy barely moves (mostly within ±1 pp): flips go both ways and land mostly on items the model gets wrong.\n  Placebo check: Llama chat templates strip trailing newlines, so the token sequence is unchanged and the flip rate is exactly 0.\n\n'''+s[b:]\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\np='planning.md'; s=open(p).read()\ns+='''\n## Deviations from the plan (recorded after execution)\n- No API keys on this host, so all models are open weights. Final set: 11 models, Qwen2.5 0.5–14B AWQ, Llama-3.2-1B/3B,\n  Llama-3.1-8B-AWQ, Qwen3-0.6B/4B-AWQ, and Qwen3-8B via the local vLLM server. Qwen3-0.6B/4B were added mid-run to get a\n  third same-family size series.\n- Final perturbations: whitespace = trailing_space, crlf, double_space, trailing_newlines, nbsp (classification) or\n  tabs (code); punctuation = curly_quotes, curly_apostrophe, unicode_dash, ellipsis_char, punct_all.\n  `ellipsis_char` applies to few items. Unchanged prompts are excluded.\n- Added a replication run (Qwen2.5-0.5B in a separate engine): all outputs were byte-identical.\n- Mixed models: GEE logistic (exchangeable, clustered by item) instead of a mixed-effects GLM.\n'''\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ncat pyproject.toml | sed -n '/dependencies/,/]/p'","description":"Update README findings and planning deviations"}}}
