{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgXF8HzEbTvxE3cBmmFHm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NKDEj2zv1Bx3kTAeWPBehY","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat >> /home/summereunann/.claude/projects/-data/memory/neurico-host-local-llm-fallback.md << 'EOF'\n\nUpdate 2026-10-29 (invisible_formatting_effects workspace, 11 models × 20k prompts, vLLM 0.32): `uv add vllm` took ~20 min (load avg 230–340, slow file copy) — start it first thing. GPU0 briefly had 18 GB free at 13:26 and then 20 GB at 14:49 — launching a worker immediately both times paid off. What worked:\n- **Worker hand-over without losing the slice** (to change code, queue order or registry): the new worker gets `TAKEOVER_PID=<old>`, finishes its imports, writes a ready file, busy-waits until /proc/<old> disappears, then creates its CUDA context and grabs 512 MB chunks. Kill the old PID in a *separate* call once the ready file exists. This kept 5/5 GB and 15/15 GB. It was used to move 14B code generation behind 7B/8B, triggered automatically by a log line (\"cls 14400/14400\").\n- Old workers re-read the shared queue file but keep their imported MODELS registry → adding new keys to that queue would KeyError them. Give new workers their own queue file.\n- The :8011 Qwen3-8B server was fast at first (~3.5k short chat requests in 90 s), then other users loaded it (48 running + 63 waiting), and the last ~2.5k 512-token code requests took ~2 h.\n- Re-running the same checkpoint in a separate engine (same prompts, same shuffle) reproduced 19,968/19,968 outputs byte-identically; a byte-identical prompt at a different batch position changed 2–21% of 512-token code outputs but ~0.02% of short labels.\n- Qwen2.5-14B-AWQ on a 15 GB slice of a 100%-util GPU: ~5 min per 1.5k short prompts, ~13 min per 1.5k 512-token code prompts.\nEOF\nsed -i 's/^- \\[Neurico host: local LLM fallback\\](neurico-host-local-llm-fallback.md) — .*/- [Neurico host: l